00:00:53.880 --> 00:01:06.180
Back in 2018, Andrés Manuel López Obrador,&nbsp;
or AMLO, was elected president of Mexico.
 &nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.080
And not only that. This former mayor of&nbsp;
the country's capital, who had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.000
run for the presidency in 2006 and 2012,&nbsp;
not only won the presidential elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.520
but also, for the first time since 1988, the&nbsp;
president's party also won a majority in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.360
Senate and the Chamber of Deputies.
 
And so, the United Mexican States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.720
were almost completely under the&nbsp;
control of AMLO and his party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.360
the National Regeneration Movement or MORENA.
 
The victory of AMLO, who presented himself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.380
the man of the people, put an end to&nbsp;
the political binomial that dominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:47.400
Mexican politics for almost 90 years.
 
Between 1929 and 2000, the Mexican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:53.460
government was in the hands of the Institutional&nbsp;
Revolutionary Party, the PRI. In 2000, Vicente&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.500
Fox of the National Action Party managed to break&nbsp;
that monopoly. He was followed by Felipe Calderón,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.880
another politician from the same party. And&nbsp;
it was only in 2012 that the PRI regained the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.140
government under Enrique Peña Nieto.
 
Even so, it was not a big change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.120
at least not a change as pronounced as&nbsp;
the victory of AMLO and MORENA in 2018.
 &nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.540
Because the truth is that the current&nbsp;
Mexican president essentially came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.920
to power with three major promises:
 
First and foremost, to be the people's president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.600
The president who would put the poor first and&nbsp;
end the corruption of the big elites once and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.760
all. We're talking about guys like, for example,&nbsp;
the former governor of Veracruz, Javier Duarte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.680
who allegedly stole almost $3 billion.
 
Second, to tackle poverty and third,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.280
to confront the enormous wave of&nbsp;
violence that the country was suffering.
 &nbsp;

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.440
After coming to power, AMLO suspended the&nbsp;
construction of the new Mexico City airport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.940
created a new National Guard, suspended&nbsp;
the granting of new oil licenses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.260
renewable energy auctions, and above all&nbsp;
launched a huge social policy package:

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.740
For example, he has raised the minimum&nbsp;
wage, increased gas and electricity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.420
subsidies and channeled billions in direct&nbsp;
transfers to more than 10 million Mexicans.&nbsp;

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:13.860
"During the neoliberal governments,&nbsp;
humble people were not taken care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.400
of. Now everyone is attended to, but preference&nbsp;
is given to the poor and the indigenous"

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.840
Thus, despite a faltering economy and the fact&nbsp;
that the oil company PEMEX, the once crown jewel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.700
of the Mexican state, is now on the ropes and that&nbsp;
violence remains out of control, the truth is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.340
AMLO has maintained very high approval ratings.
 
Basically, López Obrador is one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.920
popular leaders on the planet.&nbsp;
We are talking about approval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.840
ratings between 60% and 70%. Check it out.
So in this video we want to focus on one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.340
the great projects of this president, a great&nbsp;
investment with which he intends to revitalize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:49.320
one of the poorest regions of the country.
 
We are talking, of course, about the Tren Maya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.360
or Maya Train, a mammoth work that will cost&nbsp;
close to 20 billion dollars, that will cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.800
the jungle and that was conceived with the promise&nbsp;
of doing nothing short of transforming Mexico.
 &nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:17.340
Listen up.
 
(CROSSING THE JUNGLE)&nbsp;

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:27.060
This is Andrés Manuel López Obrador's political&nbsp;
diamond, the emblematic project that will shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.200
his legacy. A great work with which the&nbsp;
president aspires to resuscitate one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.800
poorest regions of the country economically.
 
This project is so important to the Mexican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.340
president that in 2021 López Obrador went so far&nbsp;
as to declare it a matter of national security. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.660
did so in order to be able to resume the works&nbsp;
after a court had suspended them for possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.520
violation of environmental impact legislation.
 
And then, when this decree was declared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.380
unconstitutional, AMLO again published&nbsp;
another revised decree in which he insisted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.480
on exactly the same thing: the Mayan train is&nbsp;
infrastructure that affects national security and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.100
the public interest. A project whose direction and&nbsp;
supervision, because it affects national security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:10.680
falls to the armed forces themselves.
 
(18 May 2023: AMLO issues new decree for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.880
infrastructure works to be considered&nbsp;
national security – El Economista)
 &nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.100
("We are resorting to a procedure established by&nbsp;
law, which is to declare this work of national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:24.300
security, for many reasons: because a&nbsp;
foreign government (US) is interfering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.840
because money is being lost from the&nbsp;
budget, because it is a priority work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.440
because delaying tactics are being&nbsp;
applied and because there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.640
quick justice"– Andrés Manuel López Obrador)
 
VisualPolitik viewers, with a length of 1,525&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:41.580
kilometers or 948 miles, the Mayan Train will&nbsp;
connect the states of Quintana Roo, Chiapas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.740
Tabasco, Campeche and Yucatan. One of the&nbsp;
most important tourist regions in the country.&nbsp;

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.320
This is by far the most ambitious rail&nbsp;
line to be built in Mexico in decades.
 &nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.040
It will connect the main cities and&nbsp;
tourist regions of the Yucatan Peninsula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.000
from the white sandy beaches of Cancun&nbsp;
to the archaeological remains of Tulum.
 &nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.620
As a result, the government expects this train&nbsp;
to increase tourism revenues by 20% and help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.480
create more than one million jobs. This would&nbsp;
help reduce poverty in the Yucatan Peninsula. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:16.860
region where some of the poorest states in all&nbsp;
of Mexico are located. For example, in states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:23.280
such as Chiapas and Tabasco, more than 50% of the&nbsp;
population lives in poverty or extreme poverty.
 &nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.900
But...Perhaps the biggest problem is that&nbsp;
the cost of this project has skyrocketed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.280
The initial estimate was around 6.5 billion&nbsp;
dollars, then it was announced that it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:37.140
slightly exceed 11 billion. And now, when there&nbsp;
are still many months to go before the work is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:42.900
due to be completed, they are already talking&nbsp;
about $20 billion. So it is possible that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:48.840
final figure will be even higher.("Mexico's&nbsp;
Mayan train project will cost as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.580
$20 billion, 70% over budget – Bloomberg)
 
The thing is, we are not only talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.440
AMLO's legacy, but also about one of the most&nbsp;
controversial projects in Mexico. And, guess what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:03.120
this is the crucial moment of this video:
 
What will the Maya Train really entail?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.440
What are the main arguments for and against this&nbsp;
colossal piece of infrastructure? Does it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.780
make sense? How is it possible that a train,&nbsp;
a simple train, has become so controversial?
 &nbsp;

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:16.800
Well VisualPolitik viewers, let's answer&nbsp;
all these questions. Let's take a closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.420
look at the pros and cons, the threats&nbsp;
and opportunities of AMLO's great dream.
 &nbsp;

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:31.740
(LIGHTS AND SHADOWS)
 
A train from Mexico for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:36.120
Mexico. This is the government's objective&nbsp;
and that is why both the train line and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.120
42 trains that will operate on it have been&nbsp;
designed and built in the country itself.&nbsp;

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.475
"We are building the Mayan Train, 1550 km, and the&nbsp;
trains are being built here, in Ciudad Sahagún,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.475 --> 00:07:49.020
Hidalgo, to provide jobs for&nbsp;
Mexicans". Andrés Manuel López&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:55.020
ObradorActually, they were built by the&nbsp;
French company Alstom. [So, I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.020 --> 00:08:00.660
it would be better to say that it is a train sort&nbsp;
of from Mexico for Mexico, don't you think?]
 &nbsp;

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.120
But, whatever the case, that's&nbsp;
just a small curiosity.
 &nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.380
The important thing is that the government&nbsp;
expects this train to be used by more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:13.020
8,000 travelers per day, more than 3 million&nbsp;
passengers each year. Okay, that's not a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:17.940
high figure, but this rail line will not only&nbsp;
carry passengers but also freight. And perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:21.120
this is one of the big key factors.
 
In fact, if we review the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.080
and the arguments of those who have&nbsp;
promoted it, we can find 3 key points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:30.120
the 3 major arguments, the 3 major changes&nbsp;
that this new train is expected to produce.&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:39.120
Firstly, tourism is very important for&nbsp;
Mexico and is expected to become much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.680
more so in the coming years. Both in terms&nbsp;
of domestic and international tourism. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.640
with the arrival of this new railroad, the&nbsp;
government expects that the southeastern states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:52.380
will have a much more attractive tourist offer&nbsp;
and that this will boost the economy and help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:57.000
create hundreds of thousands of jobs over time.
 
So basically it’s hoped that this train will set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.920
in motion something of a Mexican railroad&nbsp;
gold rush. In fact, government estimates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:32.940
speak of no fewer than one million jobs.
 
"Around 10 million tourists come to Cancun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:43.877
but they only come to enjoy the beaches, the&nbsp;
Caribbean Sea, and do not go to the southeastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.877 --> 00:09:43.967
states. Where there is this extraordinary,&nbsp;
cultural richness. The archaeological zones of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.967 --> 00:09:45.300
this region are the most beautiful archaeological&nbsp;
zones in the world"But that is not all. Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:48.120
AMLO argues that this work&nbsp;
is essentially a historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:53.340
reparation.
 
You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.520
traditionally most of Mexico's wealth&nbsp;
has been concentrated in the capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.660
city and in the northern states, which are&nbsp;
logically the closest to the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:04.380
This has meant that the infrastructure&nbsp;
in the south is typically underfunded.
 &nbsp;

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:08.400
Under this bonus, the aim of the Mayan&nbsp;
Train would be to correct this historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:13.080
deficiency and allow many more businesses and&nbsp;
companies to emerge in this region where more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.280
than 13 million people live.
 
("This is an act of justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.740
because this region has been the most&nbsp;
abandoned" – Andrés Manuel López Obrador)
 &nbsp;

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.000
Which brings us directly to the third&nbsp;
argument supporting this project: commodities.&nbsp;

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:39.171
Freight train, this is very important! Because&nbsp;
now a lot of merchandise is moved by trucks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.171 --> 00:10:41.425
it costs a lot to transport cargo. Costs&nbsp;
are cheaper to transport cargo by train

00:10:41.426 --> 00:10:48.540
The Tren Maya has been sold as a great tourist&nbsp;
attraction, but in reality it is, first and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:53.160
foremost, a freight train. This is the part that&nbsp;
is expected to really make money for the project.
 &nbsp;

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.700
However, when we talk about goods,&nbsp;
we are mainly talking about PEMEX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:02.520
the large state-owned oil company that accounts&nbsp;
for 80% of all cargo moved in the region. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.480
other words, PEMEX and not tourism will&nbsp;
be the main client of this project.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:11.580
So then, where does the controversy arise?&nbsp;
I don't know, in theory the advantages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:15.180
seem pretty clear, don't you think?
 
Boosting tourism, overcoming a historic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:19.440
infrastructure gap and the possibility that&nbsp;
some of Mexico's poorest states will now have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.860
a backbone for freight transportation,&nbsp;
which, of course, could encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:26.880
many companies to relocate to the region.
 
Well, VisualPolitik viewers, if we have seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:32.700
three great positives, we can also find three more&nbsp;
great disadvantages. Three major criticisms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:37.740
are made of this mammoth project. And guess what?
 
Right with the goods we came across the first one:&nbsp;

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:46.200
You see, the problem is that the Yucatan&nbsp;
peninsula is not very industrialized and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:50.640
in addition, the Tren Maya is not – at least&nbsp;
for now – connected to the rest of the network.
 &nbsp;

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:55.620
In other words, it will basically depend on&nbsp;
one customer (PEMEX) and on the return trips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.620 --> 00:12:00.180
once the goods are delivered, the rail cars and&nbsp;
containers may have to be practically empty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:04.200
This would raise the price of transportation&nbsp;
considerably and make it less competitive.
 &nbsp;

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.860
However, by far the most significant&nbsp;
criticisms are the following two.&nbsp;

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:12.660
For example, do you remember that the cost of&nbsp;
the work has already gone up to 20 billion? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.080
keep in mind that the Mexican Institute&nbsp;
for Competitiveness points out that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:21.360
figure is very likely to be too low. They&nbsp;
are pointing at more than 25 billion.
 &nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:29.220
In fact, many critics argue that this&nbsp;
enormous amount of money is being spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:32.700
to promote tourism in a region where&nbsp;
tourism is already booming. We are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:36.960
talking about such well-known places&nbsp;
as Cancun, Puerto del Carmen and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:41.580
Riviera Maya. In this way there are many&nbsp;
economists, politicians and experts who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:45.900
point out that investing all this money in&nbsp;
other more local infrastructure, in digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:49.800
infrastructure or in educational facilities&nbsp;
could give better results in the long run.
 &nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:58.080
And not only that. The Mexican State closes&nbsp;
its accounts in the red every year. In 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:03.300
for example, they had a deficit of close to 4%&nbsp;
of GDP and in 2023 they are expected to close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:08.580
with 3.6%. This means that the train will&nbsp;
have to be financed with debt. And what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:13.260
the problem with that? The Mexican government is&nbsp;
issuing its 10-year debt at rates of around 8%.
 &nbsp;

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:18.840
This means that if, in the end, the cost of&nbsp;
the project was in fact 20 billion dollars, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:24.060
Mexican State, devaluations aside, could end&nbsp;
up having to pay more than 1.6 billion dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:28.440
each year in interest alone. Naturally on&nbsp;
top of that there would be the operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:32.820
costs. And all these factors together is&nbsp;
what explains headlines like this one:
 &nbsp;

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:38.580
(The Mayan Train will never make a profit and will&nbsp;
always need government subsidies: PAN. – InfoBae)

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:42.120
Of course, supporters of the project&nbsp;
are confident that freight will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:46.320
the train profitable. And in any case, they are&nbsp;
betting that the economic activity generated in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.040
the region could far outweigh any concrete&nbsp;
losses. For example, we are talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:54.180
the one million new jobs predicted by&nbsp;
the government. Will this be possible?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:58.380
Only time will tell. For now, if one thing is&nbsp;
clear, it is that the numbers are ambitious.
 &nbsp;

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:08.100
Be that as it may, we have yet to see the last&nbsp;
major criticism. A criticism that has nothing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:13.140
do with money, but with something else entirely:
 
(4 Feb 2023:Worries abound that Mexico's Maya&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:17.040
Train will destroy jungle. – AP News)
The Mayan Train crosses and connects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.620
incredible places. We are talking, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:24.240
about the second largest Mayan jungle in the&nbsp;
Americas, second only to the Amazon, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.640
inhabited by endangered species such as jaguars&nbsp;
and the Central American tapir, among others.
 &nbsp;

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.480
Well, one third of the entire route will&nbsp;
